 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.07 — Geodesics on Surfaces\n\nWhat does \"straight\" mean on a curved surface?\n\nA straight line in the plane is a curve with zero acceleration. On a surface that is impossible: a curve confined to a sphere *must* accelerate, or it would leave the sphere. But 3.04 showed that acceleration splits into two parts,\n\n$$\\gamma'' = \\underbrace{(\\gamma'')^{\\top}}_{\\text{tangential}} + \\underbrace{\\kappa_n\\, n}_{\\text{normal}},$$\n\nand only the normal part is forced on us by the constraint. So:\n\n> A **geodesic** is a curve whose acceleration is purely normal — it does not turn *within* the surface, only as much as the surface itself compels.\n\nThe tangential part has a name: the **geodesic curvature** $\\kappa_g$, measuring turning as felt by the ant. Then\n\n$$\\kappa^2 = \\kappa_g^2 + \\kappa_n^2,$$\n\nsplitting the total curvature of a curve into the part it chooses ($\\kappa_g$) and the part the surface imposes ($\\kappa_n$). Geodesics are exactly the curves with $\\kappa_g \\equiv 0$.\n\n**The equations.** Writing the curve as $(u^1(t), u^2(t))$ in coordinates, the condition becomes\n\n$$\\ddot u^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\,\\dot u^i \\dot u^j = 0, \\qquad k = 1, 2.$$\n\nOnly the **Christoffel symbols** appear — and 3.06 established that those depend on $E, F, G$ alone. So:\n\n> **Geodesics are intrinsic.** The ant can find them without knowing the surface is curved in space.\n\nTwo characterisations coincide, and it is worth keeping both in mind:\n\n- **straightest** — zero geodesic curvature, the definition above;\n- **shortest** — locally length-minimising, from the calculus of variations.\n\nThey agree because the Euler–Lagrange equations for arc length reduce to the same system. Stage 4.03 proves this properly; here we take it and compute.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s,var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\nts(e)       := trigsimp(e)$\n\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\n\n/* Christoffel symbols from the metric alone (3.06) */\nchristoffel(I1, vars) := block([ginv],\n  ginv : ratsimp(invert(I1)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(I1[j][l], vars[i])\n                        + diff(I1[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(I1[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, 2)/2 ),\n    j,1,2), i,1,2), k,1,2) )$\n\n/* The geodesic equations, as symbolic ODEs */\ngeodesic_eqs(I1, vars) := block([G, eqs],\n  G : christoffel(I1, vars),\n  makelist(\n    'diff(vars[k], t, 2)\n      + sum( sum( G[k][i][j]*'diff(vars[i],t)*'diff(vars[j],t), j,1,2), i,1,2),\n    k, 1, 2) )$\n\nassume(R > 0, a > 0, b > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\n\n/* --- The plane: geodesics are straight lines --- */\nIpl : matrix([1,0],[0,1])$\nprint(\"plane, I = \", Ipl)$\nprint(\"   Christoffel symbols: \", christoffel(Ipl, [u,v]))$\nprint(\"   geodesic equations : \", geodesic_eqs(Ipl, [u,v]), \" = 0\")$\nprint(\"   ⟹ ü = v̈ = 0, so u and v are linear in t: straight lines ✓\")$\n\n/* --- The sphere --- */\nsphere : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\nIsph : FFF(sphere,u,v)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"sphere, I = \", Isph)$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", christoffel(Isph,[u,v])[1])$\nprint(\"   Γ² = \", christoffel(Isph,[u,v])[2])$\nprint(\"   geodesic equations:\")$\nfor e in geodesic_eqs(Isph,[u,v]) do print(\"      \", e, \" = 0\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "plane, I =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n   Christoffel symbols:  [[[0,0],[0,0]],[[0,0],[0,0]]]\n   geodesic equations :  ['diff(u,t,2),'diff(v,t,2)]  = 0\n   ⟹ ü = v̈ = 0, so u and v are linear in t: straight lines ✓\nsphere, I =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,-(cos(u)*sin(u))]]\n   Γ² =  [[0,cos(u)/sin(u)],[cos(u)/sin(u),0]]\n   geodesic equations:\n       'diff(u,t,2)-cos(u)*sin(u)*('diff(v,t,1))^2  = 0\n       'diff(v,t,2)+(2*cos(u)*'diff(u,t,1)*'diff(v,t,1))/sin(u)  = 0"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Substitute an explicit curve (u(t), v(t)) into the sphere's geodesic\n   equations and see whether both residuals vanish. */\ncheck(uf, vf, label) := block([r1, r2],\n  r1 : ts(ratsimp( diff(uf,t,2) - cos(uf)*sin(uf)*diff(vf,t)^2 )),\n  r2 : ts(ratsimp( diff(vf,t,2) + 2*cos(uf)/sin(uf)*diff(uf,t)*diff(vf,t) )),\n  print(\"   \", label),\n  print(\"      residual 1 = \", r1, \"     residual 2 = \", r2),\n  print(\"      \", if r1 = 0 and r2 = 0 then \"GEODESIC ✓\" else \"NOT a geodesic\") )$\n\nprint(\"candidate curves on the sphere:\")$\nprint(\"\")$\ncheck( t, 0,      \"meridian: u = t, v = 0 (a great circle through the poles)\" )$\nprint(\"\")$\ncheck( %pi/2, t,  \"equator: u = π/2, v = t (a great circle)\" )$\nprint(\"\")$\ncheck( %pi/6, t,  \"parallel at colatitude π/6: u = π/6, v = t (a small circle)\" )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so meridians and the equator are geodesics, but other parallels are not —\")$\nprint(\"exactly the great-circle result of 2.07, now derived from the metric alone.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "candidate curves on the sphere:\n    meridian: u = t, v = 0 (a great circle through the poles)\n      residual 1 =  0      residual 2 =  0\n       GEODESIC ✓\n    equator: u = π/2, v = t (a great circle)\n      residual 1 =  0      residual 2 =  0\n       GEODESIC ✓\n    parallel at colatitude π/6: u = π/6, v = t (a small circle)\n      residual 1 =  -(sqrt(3)/4)      residual 2 =  0\n       NOT a geodesic\nso meridians and the equator are geodesics, but other parallels are not —\nexactly the great-circle result of 2.07, now derived from the metric alone."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Clairaut's relation\n\nOn a surface of revolution the geodesic equations have a conserved quantity, and it is both useful and geometrically vivid.\n\nTake $\\sigma = (f(u)\\cos v,\\, f(u)\\sin v,\\, g(u))$, so $\\mathrm{I} = \\mathrm{diag}(E, f^2)$ with $E = f'^2 + g'^2$. The coordinate $v$ does not appear in the metric — the surface is rotationally symmetric — and the second geodesic equation becomes a conservation law:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(f^2\\,\\dot v\\right) = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f^2\\,\\dot v = \\text{const}.$$\n\nWriting $\\psi$ for the angle between the geodesic and the meridian, this is\n\n$$\\boxed{\\;f\\,\\sin\\psi = \\text{const}\\;}$$\n\n**Clairaut's relation** (1733). In words: *the further a geodesic strays from the axis, the more nearly it runs along a meridian*.\n\n**Why the conservation law exists.** Because $v$ is a **cyclic coordinate** — the metric does not depend on it. That is Noether's theorem in its simplest form: a symmetry of the metric (here, rotation about the axis) yields a conserved quantity along geodesics. In Stage 4 such a symmetry is called a *Killing field*, and in 6.02 exactly this argument produces the conserved energy and angular momentum of orbits around a black hole — which is how the perihelion precession of Mercury is computed.\n\n**What it predicts.** On a surface of revolution a geodesic starting at radius $f_0$ with angle $\\psi_0$ satisfies $f\\sin\\psi = f_0\\sin\\psi_0$. Since $\\sin\\psi \\le 1$, the geodesic can never reach a radius smaller than $f_0\\sin\\psi_0$ — it is turned back by a **barrier**. On a torus this traps some geodesics in the outer region forever; on a hyperboloid it keeps them away from the throat.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Clairaut: the second geodesic equation is a conservation law --- */\ndepends([f,g], u)$\nsrev  : [f*cos(v), f*sin(v), g]$\nIsrev : FFF(srev, u, v)$\nprint(\"surface of revolution, I = \", Isrev)$\nGsr : christoffel(Isrev, [u,v])$\nprint(\"   Γ² = \", Gsr[2])$\n\neq2 : geodesic_eqs(Isrev, [u,v])[2]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"second geodesic equation: \", eq2, \" = 0\")$\n\n/* Multiply by f² and it becomes d/dt(f² v̇) */\ndepends([u,v], t)$\ncons_law : ratsimp( diff( f^2*diff(v,t), t ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"d/dt (f² v̇) = \", cons_law)$\nprint(\"f² × (second equation) = \", ratsimp( f^2 * eq2 ))$\nprint(\"difference = \", ratsimp( cons_law - f^2*eq2 ),\n      \"   ⟹ f² v̇ is conserved ∎\")$\n\n/* --- The barrier, on a torus --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Clairaut on a torus with a = 3, b = 1 (radius f = 3 + cos u):\")$\nprint(\"   radius runs from \", 3-1, \" (inner equator) to \", 3+1, \" (outer equator)\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   start at the outer equator (f₀ = 4) heading at angle ψ₀:\")$\nprint(\"      ψ₀        f₀ sin ψ₀      minimum radius reachable   crosses to inside?\")$\nfor psi0 in [%pi/2, %pi/3, %pi/4, %pi/6, %pi/12] do block([c],\n  c : float(4*sin(psi0)),\n  print(\"     \", float(psi0), \"     \", c, \"        \", c, \"            \",\n        if c > 2 then \"NO — trapped outside\" else \"yes\") )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"a geodesic launched close enough to the parallel direction can never\")$\nprint(\"reach the inner equator: the barrier f ≥ f₀ sin ψ₀ forbids it.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "surface of revolution, I =\n                           matrix([('diff(g,u,1))^2+('diff(f,u,1))^2,0],\n                                  [0,f^2])\n   Γ² =  [[0,'diff(f,u,1)/f],['diff(f,u,1)/f,0]]\nsecond geodesic equation:\n                          'diff(v,t,2)+(2*'diff(f,u,1)*'diff(u,t,1)\n                                         *'diff(v,t,1))\n                                       /f  = 0\nd/dt (f² v̇) =  f^2*'diff(v,t,2)+2*f*'diff(f,u,1)*'diff(u,t,1)*'diff(v,t,1)\nf² × (second equation) =\n                         f^2*'diff(v,t,2)\n                          +2*f*'diff(f,u,1)*'diff(u,t,1)*'diff(v,t,1)\ndifference =  0    ⟹ f² v̇ is conserved ∎\nClairaut on a torus with a = 3, b = 1 (radius f = 3 + cos u):\n   radius runs from  2  (inner equator) to  4  (outer equator)\n   start at the outer equator (f₀ = 4) heading at angle ψ₀:\n      ψ₀        f₀ sin ψ₀      minimum radius reachable   crosses to inside?\n      1.5707963267948966       4.0          4.0\n     NO — trapped outside\n      1.0471975511965976       3.4641016151377544          3.4641016151377544\n                  NO — trapped outside\n      0.7853981633974483       2.8284271247461907          2.8284271247461907\n                  NO — trapped outside\n      0.5235987755982988       2.0          2.0              yes\n      0.2617993877991494       1.035276180410083          1.035276180410083\n                  yes\na geodesic launched close enough to the parallel direction can never\nreach the inner equator: the barrier f ≥ f₀ sin ψ₀ forbids it."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Geodesics on a torus, integrated numerically --- */\nremove([u,v], dependency)$\ntorus : [(3 + cos(u))*cos(v), (3 + cos(u))*sin(v), sin(u)]$\nItor  : FFF(torus, u, v)$\nGtor  : christoffel(Itor, [u,v])$\nprint(\"torus a = 3, b = 1:   I = \", Itor)$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", Gtor[1])$\nprint(\"   Γ² = \", Gtor[2])$\n\n/* Extract the two nonzero symbols as Maxima functions of u */\nG122(uu) := float( subst(u = uu, Gtor[1][2][2]) )$\nG212(uu) := float( subst(u = uu, Gtor[2][1][2]) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Γ¹₂₂(u) = \", Gtor[1][2][2], \"     Γ²₁₂(u) = \", Gtor[2][1][2])$\n\n/* State y = [u, v, u̇, v̇];  ü = −Γ¹₂₂ v̇²,  v̈ = −2Γ²₁₂ u̇ v̇ */\ngeo_rhs(y) := block([uu, du, dv],\n  uu : y[1], du : y[3], dv : y[4],\n  [ du, dv, -G122(uu)*dv^2, -2*G212(uu)*du*dv ] )$\n\nrk4(y, h) := block([k1,k2,k3,k4],\n  k1 : geo_rhs(y), k2 : geo_rhs(y + (h/2)*k1),\n  k3 : geo_rhs(y + (h/2)*k2), k4 : geo_rhs(y + h*k3),\n  y + (h/6)*(k1 + 2*k2 + 2*k3 + k4) )$\n\n/* Launch from the outer equator (u = 0, radius 4) at angle ψ₀ to the parallel.\n   Unit speed: E u̇² + G v̇² = 1, with E = 1, G = (3+cos u)². */\nlaunch(psi0) := block([f0],\n  f0 : 4.0,\n  [0.0, 0.0, float(cos(psi0)), float(sin(psi0)/f0)] )$\n\nrun(psi0, nsteps, h) := block([y, path],\n  y : launch(psi0), path : [[y[1], y[2]]],\n  for i : 1 thru nsteps do ( y : rk4(y, h), path : cons([y[1],y[2]], path) ),\n  reverse(path) )$\n\n/* Clairaut invariant f sin ψ should stay constant along the flow */\nclairaut(y) := block([f_], f_ : 3 + cos(y[1]), float( f_^2 * y[4] ))$\n\nfor psi0 in [%pi/3, %pi/8] do block([y, c0, cN],\n  y : launch(psi0), c0 : clairaut(y),\n  for i : 1 thru 4000 do y : rk4(y, 0.01),\n  cN : clairaut(y),\n  print(\"\"),\n  print(\"launch angle ψ₀ = \", float(psi0)),\n  print(\"   Clairaut f²v̇ at start = \", c0, \"   after 4000 steps = \", cN),\n  print(\"   drift = \", cN - c0, \"   — conserved ✓\"),\n  print(\"   colatitude u reached: \", float(y[1]), \"   (mod 2π: \",\n        float(mod(y[1], 2*%pi)), \")\") )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "torus a = 3, b = 1:   I =  matrix([1,0],[0,cos(u)^2+6*cos(u)+9])\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,(cos(u)+3)*sin(u)]]\n   Γ² =  [[0,-(sin(u)/(cos(u)+3))],[-(sin(u)/(cos(u)+3)),0]]\n   Γ¹₂₂(u) =  (cos(u)+3)*sin(u)      Γ²₁₂(u) =  -(sin(u)/(cos(u)+3))\nlaunch angle ψ₀ =  1.0471975511965976\n   Clairaut f²v̇ at start =  3.4641016151377544    after 4000 steps =\n                            3.4641016151381576\n   drift =  4.0323300254385686e-13    — conserved ✓\n   colatitude u reached:  0.03314048150840722    (mod 2π:  0.03314048150840722\n                          )\nlaunch angle ψ₀ =  0.39269908169872414\n   Clairaut f²v̇ at start =  1.5307337294603591    after 4000 steps =\n                            1.5307337294558656\n   drift =  -4.4935166698678586e-12    — conserved ✓\n   colatitude u reached:  32.646845741930406    (mod 2π:  1.2309192060324747 )"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Two geodesics on the torus, drawn in the (v, u) parameter domain.\n   u is the angle around the tube, v the angle around the axis. */\np_trapped : run(%pi/3, 3000, 0.01)$\np_free    : run(%pi/8, 3000, 0.01)$\n\nband(psi0) := block([c], c : 4.0*float(sin(psi0)),\n  /* f = 3 + cos u ≥ c  ⟹  cos u ≥ c − 3 */\n  if c - 3 > 1 then \"unreachable\"\n  elseif c - 3 < -1 then \"no barrier — the whole tube is reachable\"\n  else float(acos(c - 3)) )$\n\nprint(\"barrier colatitude |u| ≤ arccos(f₀sinψ₀ − 3):\")$\nprint(\"   ψ₀ = π/3 : f₀sinψ₀ = \", float(4*sin(%pi/3)),\n      \"   → |u| ≤ \", band(%pi/3), \"  (trapped near the outer equator)\")$\nprint(\"   ψ₀ = π/8 : f₀sinψ₀ = \", float(4*sin(%pi/8)),\n      \"   → \", band(%pi/8))$\n\nub : band(%pi/3)$\nplot2d([ [discrete, map(second, p_trapped), map(first, p_trapped)],\n         [discrete, map(second, p_free),    map(first, p_free)],\n         [discrete, [0.0, 12.0], [ub, ub]],\n         [discrete, [0.0, 12.0], [-ub, -ub]] ],\n       [x, 0, 12], [y, -4, 8],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,2],[lines,1],[lines,1]],\n       [legend, \"ψ₀ = π/3 — trapped\", \"ψ₀ = π/8 — crosses the tube\",\n                \"Clairaut barrier\", \"Clairaut barrier\"],\n       [xlabel, \"v — angle around the axis\"],\n       [ylabel, \"u — angle around the tube\"],\n       [title, \"geodesics on a torus: one bounded by Clairaut, one not\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 6,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "barrier colatitude |u| ≤ arccos(f₀sinψ₀ − 3):\n   ψ₀ = π/3 : f₀sinψ₀ =  3.4641016151377544    → |u| ≤  1.0881762133641693\n                          (trapped near the outer equator)\n   ψ₀ = π/8 : f₀sinψ₀ =  1.5307337294603591    →\n                        no barrier — the whole tube is reachable"
     ]
