  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\ddot u^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\,\\dot u^i\\dot u^j = 0$$\n\n| Surface | Geodesics |\n|---|---|\n| plane | $\\ddot u = \\ddot v = 0$ — straight lines |\n| sphere | great circles (meridians and the equator verified; other parallels fail) |\n| cylinder | helices, including lines and circles as limiting cases |\n| surface of revolution | governed by Clairaut: $f\\sin\\psi = \\text{const}$ |\n| torus | no closed form — integrated numerically |\n\nVerified: the parallel at colatitude $\\pi/6$ has residual $-\\sqrt3/4 \\ne 0$, so it is not a geodesic, while meridians and the equator are; $f^2\\dot v$ is exactly conserved, $d/dt(f^2\\dot v) - f^2\\cdot(\\text{eq. 2}) = 0$; and along numerically integrated torus geodesics the Clairaut invariant drifted by $4\\times10^{-13}$ over 4000 RK4 steps.\n\n**The barrier is real.** A geodesic launched from the outer equator of the torus ($f_0 = 4$) at $\\psi_0 = \\pi/3$ has $f\\sin\\psi = 3.46$, so it can only visit radii $\\ge 3.46$ — colatitudes $|u| \\le 1.088$. The numerics confirm it: after 4000 steps it was still at $u = 0.033$, oscillating in its band. Launched at $\\psi_0 = \\pi/8$ the invariant is $1.53 < 2$, no barrier exists, and it had wound to $u = 32.6$ — more than five times round the tube.\n\n## Three things this sets up\n\n**Geodesics are intrinsic**, because the equations contain only Christoffel symbols and 3.06 showed those come from the metric. The ant finds the same \"straight lines\" as an observer in space. This is what makes Stage 4 possible: geodesics can be defined on a manifold with no ambient space at all.\n\n**Symmetry gives conservation.** Clairaut's relation came from $v$ being absent from the metric. Stage 4 calls this a Killing field, and 6.02 uses precisely this mechanism — the Schwarzschild metric's independence of $t$ and $\\phi$ — to extract the conserved energy and angular momentum that determine planetary orbits and light bending.\n\n**Geodesic curvature is what Gauss–Bonnet integrates.** We defined $\\kappa_g$ as the tangential part of acceleration and geodesics as $\\kappa_g \\equiv 0$. In 3.08 the boundary term of Gauss–Bonnet is $\\oint\\kappa_g\\,ds$, which vanishes exactly when the boundary is made of geodesics — which is why the theorem takes its cleanest form on geodesic triangles, and why it reproduces the angle-deficit results of 2.06 and 2.07.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.08 — Gauss–Bonnet** is the summit of the stage. It ties $\\int\\!\\!\\int K\\,dA$ and $\\oint \\kappa_g\\,ds$ to the Euler characteristic, making the total curvature of a closed surface a topological invariant — as already glimpsed when the torus integrated to zero in 3.04.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §4-4 for geodesics, §4-4 Example 5 for Clairaut's relation.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapter 9, which treats geodesics on surfaces of revolution in detail and works the torus.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapters 4–6: geodesics, the exponential map, and the equivalence of \"straightest\" and \"shortest\" — which 3.07 assumed and 4.03 will prove.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. and Leok, M. and McClamroch, N.H. (2018) *Global Formulations of Lagrangian and Hamiltonian Dynamics on Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. For the variational route to the same equations, and the mechanics connection.\n\nClairaut's relation dates from Alexis Clairaut's work on the shape of the Earth (1733).\n\n**On the exponential map.** Given $p$ and a tangent vector $X$, the geodesic starting at $p$ with velocity $X$ defines $\\exp_p(X) = \\gamma(1)$. Using it as a coordinate chart gives **normal coordinates**, in which $g_{ij}(p) = \\delta_{ij}$ and $\\Gamma^k_{ij}(p) = 0$ — the metric is flat *to first order* at $p$, and curvature appears only at second order. That construction is developed in 4.03; in relativity it is the mathematical content of the equivalence principle.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
